  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q-add-1",
      "concept_id": "con-adding-fractions",
      "stem": "What is 1/3 + 1/4?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "q-add-1-a", "text": "7/12", "correct": true, "misconception_id": null},
        {"id": "q-add-1-b", "text": "2/7", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-add-num-den"},
        {"id": "q-add-1-c", "text": "2/12", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-no-lcm"},
        {"id": "q-add-1-d", "text": "1/7", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "gap"}
      ],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "is_diagnostic": true,
      "is_transfer": false,
      "hints": [
        {"level": 1, "text": "Think about what you need to do before you can add two fractions with different bottom numbers."},
        {"level": 2, "text": "To add fractions with unlike denominators, first find the Least Common Multiple (LCM) of the denominators. The LCM of 3 and 4 is 12."},
        {"level": 3, "text": "Worked example with different numbers: 1/2 + 1/5. LCM of 2 and 5 is 10. So 1/2 = 5/10 and 1/5 = 2/10. Answer: 5/10 + 2/10 = 7/10."},
        {"level": 4, "text": "Step 1: Find LCM of 3 and 4, which is 12. Step 2: Convert 1/3 to 4/12. Step 3: Convert 1/4 to 3/12. Step 4: Add the numerators: 4 + 3 = 7. The answer is 7/12."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q-add-2",
      "concept_id": "con-adding-fractions",
      "stem": "What is 2/5 + 1/3?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "q-add-2-a", "text": "11/15", "correct": true, "misconception_id": null},
        {"id": "q-add-2-b", "text": "3/8", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-add-num-den"},
        {"id": "q-add-2-c", "text": "3/15", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-no-lcm"},
        {"id": "q-add-2-d", "text": "2/15", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "gap"}
      ],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "is_diagnostic": true,
      "is_transfer": false,
      "hints": [
        {"level": 1, "text": "What is the smallest number that both 5 and 3 divide into evenly?"},
        {"level": 2, "text": "The LCM of 5 and 3 is 15. Convert each fraction so its denominator is 15, then add the numerators."},
        {"level": 3, "text": "Worked example: 3/4 + 1/6. LCM of 4 and 6 is 12. 3/4 = 9/12 and 1/6 = 2/12. Answer: 9/12 + 2/12 = 11/12."},
        {"level": 4, "text": "Step 1: LCM of 5 and 3 is 15. Step 2: 2/5 = 6/15. Step 3: 1/3 = 5/15. Step 4: 6/15 + 5/15 = 11/15."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q-equiv-1",
      "concept_id": "con-equivalent-fractions",
      "stem": "Which fraction is equivalent to 3/4?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "q-equiv-1-a", "text": "6/8", "correct": true, "misconception_id": null},
        {"id": "q-equiv-1-b", "text": "6/4", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-mult-num-only"},
        {"id": "q-equiv-1-c", "text": "3/8", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-cross-simplify-wrong"},
        {"id": "q-equiv-1-d", "text": "4/5", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "gap"}
      ],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "is_diagnostic": true,
      "is_transfer": false,
      "hints": [
        {"level": 1, "text": "To find an equivalent fraction, you need to do the same thing to both the top and the bottom."},
        {"level": 2, "text": "Multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the same number. Try multiplying 3/4 by 2/2."},
        {"level": 3, "text": "Worked example: Find a fraction equivalent to 2/3. Multiply top and bottom by 3: 2*3 = 6, 3*3 = 9. So 6/9 is equivalent to 2/3."},
        {"level": 4, "text": "Step 1: Choose a multiplier, say 2. Step 2: Multiply numerator: 3 * 2 = 6. Step 3: Multiply denominator: 4 * 2 = 8. Answer: 6/8."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q-mult-1",
      "concept_id": "con-multiplying-fractions",
      "stem": "What is 2/3 * 3/5?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "q-mult-1-a", "text": "6/15 or 2/5", "correct": true, "misconception_id": null},
        {"id": "q-mult-1-b", "text": "5/8", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-mult-add-den"},
        {"id": "q-mult-1-c", "text": "6/8", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "gap"},
        {"id": "q-mult-1-d", "text": "2/15", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "gap"}
      ],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "is_diagnostic": true,
      "is_transfer": false,
      "hints": [
        {"level": 1, "text": "When multiplying fractions, what do you do with the numerators and what do you do with the denominators?"},
        {"level": 2, "text": "To multiply fractions, multiply the numerators together and multiply the denominators together: (a/b) * (c/d) = (a*c)/(b*d)."},
        {"level": 3, "text": "Worked example: 1/4 * 2/3 = (1*2)/(4*3) = 2/12 = 1/6."},
        {"level": 4, "text": "Step 1: Multiply numerators: 2 * 3 = 6. Step 2: Multiply denominators: 3 * 5 = 15. Step 3: Result is 6/15. Step 4: Simplify by dividing both by 3: 2/5."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q-mult-2",
      "concept_id": "con-multiplying-fractions",
      "stem": "What is 4/7 * 1/2?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "q-mult-2-a", "text": "4/14 or 2/7", "correct": true, "misconception_id": null},
        {"id": "q-mult-2-b", "text": "5/9", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "misc-mult-add-den"},
        {"id": "q-mult-2-c", "text": "4/9", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "gap"},
        {"id": "q-mult-2-d", "text": "8/7", "correct": false, "misconception_id": "gap"}
      ],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "is_diagnostic": true,
      "is_transfer": false,
      "hints": [
        {"level": 1, "text": "Remember the rule: multiply straight across, top with top, bottom with bottom."},
        {"level": 2, "text": "Multiply numerators: 4 * 1. Multiply denominators: 7 * 2. Then simplify if possible."},
        {"level": 3, "text": "Worked example: 3/5 * 2/3 = (3*2)/(5*3) = 6/15 = 2/5."},
        {"level": 4, "text": "Step 1: Numerators: 4 * 1 = 4. Step 2: Denominators: 7 * 2 = 14. Step 3: Result: 4/14. Step 4: Simplify: divide both by 2 to get 2/7."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
